From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 17:07:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] WebPlayer: keep the shared content filter lists up to date

Web apps may ask for maintained ad and tracker lists such as EasyList by
name. Kodi now keeps those lists itself: the advanced settings name them
and their URLs under <webapps><contentfilterlists>, with EasyList and
EasyPrivacy as default configuration in system/contentfilterlists.xml,
and CWebFilterLists stores them in the profile, with the ETag and
Last-Modified date of each, and hands them to the web client. The lists
installed web apps name are fetched in the background when Kodi starts,
a list named later when a web app first asks for it. As Kodi may run
for a long time, every six hours a HEAD request asks for each list's
published generation. A list states how often it is to be updated, as
"! Expires: 4 days" in its header; that interval is stored with the
Date the server sent the list with, and a newer generation is fetched
only once the server's Date in a check is that far ahead. Both times
are the server's, so a local clock that is not set yet neither
shortens nor extends the interval. A list without that header line is
fetched whenever a newer generation is published. The client is
handed each list once a run, and again when its content changes. A
fetch that fails, for instance because TLS rejects certificates
against a clock that is still wrong, is retried as soon as the system
clock is set; meanwhile the stored copy serves.

Signed-off-by: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
